ntitative investigations of this ­phenomenon46. It has\n124:                                            a randomized, controlled clinical trial in major depressive disorder, in which changes in beliefs were measured\n127:                                            Results\n128:                                            Non‑physicalist beliefs. 866 respondents completed the survey enquiring about their metaphysical beliefs\n129:                                            at different timepoints (see “Methods”, “Supplementary Results”, and Supplementary Table 1 for sample char-\n134:                                            sophical terms to the individual items and a factor dimension (Table 1) but also encourage that they be viewed\n138:                                            labelled Non-physicalist Beliefs (NPB; see “Methods” for factor analysis results). This single factor showed good\n140:                                            separate and supernatural realms of existence, a non-natural unifying principle in reality, panpsychism, dualism,\n143:                                            (naturalism). These items and their loadings are shown in Table 1.\n144:                                                We compared NPB scores before attending a ceremony involving psychedelic use (baseline) with NPB scores\n145:                                            4 weeks and 6 months after the ceremony. Pooling scores for the NPB factor, analyses revealed a significant shift\n146:                                            away from physicalism at 4 weeks compared with baseline (t(121) = 3.66, p = 0.001, d = 0.33, confidence inter-\n147:                                            val, 95% CI [0.12, 0.39]). These changes were sustained 6 months after the ceremony (t(121) = 5.07, p < 0.0001,\n160:                                  Figure 1.  Psychedelic use is associated with shifts in metaphysical beliefs away from hard physicalism or\n162:                      