                    responders in the escitalopram group (p value and Hedges’ g effect size shown). (c) Shift in non-physicalist\n291:                                            versus baseline, and this was specific for the psilocybin group (i.e., it was not seen in the escitalopram group).\n301:                                            (response is defined as at least 50% reduction in depression scores from baseline to week 6), versus those who\n303:                                            that the belief-shifts in the psilocybin condition were largely correlated with positive endorsement of a unifying\n311:                                            tional and controlled research data suggest a relationship between psychedelic experiences and shifts away from\n312:                                            positions of hard physicalism and towards panpsychism, dualistic, and fatalistic beliefs. The observed changes\n313:                                            were enduring, persisting for up to 6 months in most domains, with the exception of fatalistic determinism.\n314:                                            Moreover, the large-sample prospective/observational and smaller but well-controlled research sample findings\n316:                                            beliefs. Furthermore, the belief-shifts were correlated with positive mental health changes; namely, improvements\n317:                                            in well-being in the observational data and depression scores in the controlled research data.\n319:                                            ing ceremonial psychedelic use. Unlike non-physicalist beliefs, these changes were not sustained in time. One\n322:                                            it is plausible that non-physicalist beliefs feature more heavily in ‘psychedelic culture’, compared with beliefs\n359:                                   erated ‘emotional synchrony’60 felt during the psychedelic ceremony itself. H