{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\noutput_json_path = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/runs/run-20260426T152518Z--g2jrtnjpv1/semantic/ingest_extraction.json')\noutput_json_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\ndata = {\n  \\\"source_date_inference\\\": {\n    \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\n    \\\"date\\\": None,\n    \\\"origin\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\n    \\\"rationale\\\": None\n  },\n  \\\"source\\\": {\n    \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n    \\\"display_title\\\": \\\"Psychedelics Shift Beliefs Beyond Hard Materialism\\\",\n    \\\"authors\\\": [\n      \\\"Christopher Timmermann\\\",\n      \\\"Hannes Kettner\\\",\n      \\\"Chris Letheby\\\",\n      \\\"Leor Roseman\\\",\n      \\\"Fernando E. Rosas\\\",\n      \\\"Robin L. Carhart-Harris\\\"\n    ],\n    \\\"doi\\\": \\\"10.1038/s41598-021-01209-2\\\",\n    \\\"journal\\\": \\\"Scientific Reports\\\"\n  },\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Psychedelic use is associated with significant shifts away from hard physicalist or materialist metaphysical beliefs and toward non-physicalist positions such as panpsychism, dualism, and non-naturalism.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Psychedelics reduce hard materialism\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Pooling scores for the NPB factor, analyses revealed a significant shift away from physicalism at 4 weeks compared with baseline (t(121) = 3.66, p = 0.001, d = 0.33, 95% CI [0.12, 0.39]).\\\",\n        \\\"The Non-Physicalist Beliefs factor included positive loadings for beliefs in supernatural realms, non-natural unifying principles, panpsychism, dualism, and solipsism/idealism, while materialism and naturalism loaded negatively.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Most psychedelic-associated shifts in non-physicalist metaphysical beliefs persisted for at least six months, while fatalistic determinism was less durable in the overall observational sample.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Belief shifts partly endure\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"These changes were sustained 6 months after the ceremony (t(121) = 5.07, p < 0.0001, d = 0.46, 95% CI [0.22, 0.50]).\\\",\n        \\\"The observed changes were enduring, persisting for up to 6 months in most domains, with the exception of fatalistic determinism.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Participants with no prior psychedelic experience showed larger shifts away from physicalism than the full observational sample.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Naive users shift more\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Their first psychedelic experience ... significant changes found at 4 weeks (t(52) = 3.85, p = 0.001, d = 0.53, 95% CI [0.21, 0.66]) and 6 months (t(52) = 5.32, p < 0.0001, d = 0.73, 95% CI [0.36, 0.80]).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Greater lifetime psychedelic use correlates with stronger baseline panpsychist beliefs and weaker baseline hard materialist beliefs.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Past use predicts panpsychism\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"A significant positive correlation was found between previous psychedelic use and shifts away from the hard-materialism pole of the hard-materialism vs. hard-dualism spectrum at baseline (r = 0.223, p < 0.0001).\\\",\n        \\\"Lifetime psychedelic use was positively correlated with panpsychist views and negatively correlated with hard materialistic views measured at baseline.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Ceremonial psychedelic use increased fatalistic determinism at four weeks in the overall prospective sample, but this effect did not persist to six months except in the psychedelic-naive subgroup.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Fatalism rises temporarily\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"The psychedelic ceremony was associated with increases in scores of Fatalistic determinism at 4 weeks versus baseline (t(121) = 2.81, p = 0.012, d = 0.25, 95% CI [0.06, 0.37]); however, this effect did not persist at 6 months.\\\",\n        \\\"For psychedelic-naïve participants ... the changes persisted for at least 6 months (t(52) = 2.86, p = 0.012, d = 0.39, 95% CI [0.11, 0.64]).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Shifts away from hard materialism are positively associated with increases in psychological well-being after psychedelic ceremonies.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Belief change tracks wellbeing\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"A significant positive correlation was found between shifts away from hard-materialism (the NPB factor) and changes in well-being. The correlation was significant at 4 weeks and at 6 months post-ceremony.\\\",\n        \\\"Figure 3. Shifts away from hard materialistic beliefs are associated with increases in well-being.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Perceived emotional synchrony during the psychedelic ceremony mediates later changes in non-physicalist beliefs, with baseline peer conformity moderating that pathway.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Synchrony mediates belief change\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Results supported a model with excellent fit in which perceived ‘emotional synchrony’ with other participants—moderated by baseline scores of peer conformity—predicted subsequent changes in the NPB factor.\\\",\n        \\\"Figure 4. Changes in non-physicalist beliefs are moderated by baseline variables and pre-state identify fusion and mediated by acute emotional synchrony during the psychedelic session.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"In a randomized controlled depression trial, psilocybin therapy produced shifts away from hard materialism at six weeks whereas escitalopram did not.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Psilocybin shifts materialism\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"A significant drug versus time interaction was observed (F(56) = 3.13, p = 0.041, one-tailed).\\\",\n        \\\"Post-hoc tests reveal that shifts away from hard materialism were evident in the psilocybin group only (Z = 2.28, p = 0.02, d = 0.45). The escitalopram group showed no changes in NPB (Z = 0.24, p = 0.33, d = 0.2).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Patients who clinically responded to psilocybin therapy showed greater shifts away from hard materialist beliefs than patients who responded to escitalopram.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Psilocybin response shifts beliefs\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Significantly greater shifts away from hard materialistic beliefs were found for those patients who showed a clinically meaningful response to psilocybin only ... versus those who showed a response to escitalopram (Z = 1.74, p = 0.041, g = 0.56, 90% CI [− 0.17, 1.26]).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"The combined prospective, correlational, mechanistic, and controlled-trial evidence supports a possible causal influence of psychedelic use on metaphysical belief change, while remaining limited by sample selection and contextual confounds.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Causality remains context-limited\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"These lines of evidence include: (1) that the belief-shifts were observed prospectively ... (5) independent data from a separate controlled study in a different population, replicated our observational research findings.\\\",\n        \\\"The study did not collect respondents’ and patients’ religious affiliations at baseline, differential environmental susceptibilities, or interests concerning metaphysical beliefs.\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"evidence_items\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-nonphysicalist-factor-loadings--a15b9c27d4e8\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Non-Physicalist Factor Structure\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"A factor analysis on the belief items revealed a single Non-physicalist Beliefs factor with good internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha = 0.86). Positive loadings included separate and supernatural realms, a non-natural unifying principle, panpsychism, dualism, and solipsism/idealism; negative loadings included hard materialism, physicalism, and naturalism.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This factor-analysis result defines the main outcome variable used throughout the study. It matters because the reported belief changes are not tied to a single questionnaire item, but to a composite that contrasts non-physicalist positions with hard materialist or naturalist positions. The interpretation remains bounded by the authors’ warning that philosophical labels for items are contestable.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"0\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Table 1\\\", \\\"p.2\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-prospective-npb-shift--b621cf90a43d\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prospective Belief Shift\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Pooling scores for the NPB factor, analyses revealed a significant shift away from physicalism at 4 weeks compared with baseline (t(121) = 3.66, p = 0.001, d = 0.33, 95% CI [0.12, 0.39]). These changes were sustained 6 months after the ceremony (t(121) = 5.07, p < 0.0001, d = 0.46, 95% CI [0.22, 0.50]).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The pre-versus-post comparison is the central prospective evidence that psychedelic ceremonies were followed by measurable movement away from hard physicalism. It establishes temporal ordering and durability for the composite non-physicalist belief measure, though the observational ceremony context means it does not by itself fully isolate the pharmacological cause.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"1\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 1\\\", \\\"p.2\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-naive-participant-effects--c83d4f51e6a0\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Naive Participant Effects\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"For respondents embarking on their first psychedelic experience, significant changes were found at 4 weeks (t(52) = 3.85, p = 0.001, d = 0.53, 95% CI [0.21, 0.66]) and 6 months (t(52) = 5.32, p < 0.0001, d = 0.73, 95% CI [0.36, 0.80]).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The stronger effect sizes among psychedelic-naive participants support the interpretation that the observed shifts are not merely a residue of earlier psychedelic exposure. This evidence is especially relevant to causal interpretation, while still sharing the observational limitations of the ceremony cohort.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"2\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Supplementary Fig. 1a\\\", \\\"p.3\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-lifetime-use-correlations--d94e5a62f7b1\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Lifetime Use Correlations\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"A significant positive correlation was found between previous psychedelic use and shifts away from the hard-materialism pole of the hard-materialism vs. hard-dualism spectrum at baseline (r = 0.223, p < 0.0001). Figure 2 reports that lifetime psychedelic use was positively correlated with panpsychist views and negatively correlated with hard materialistic views measured at baseline.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The cross-sectional baseline correlation gives convergent evidence that psychedelic exposure and metaphysical orientation vary together even before the prospective ceremony interval. It is not sufficient for causation, but it reinforces the direction of the prospective findings and specifically links past exposure to panpsychist and anti-materialist endorsement.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"3\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 1\\\", \\\"Fig. 2\\\", \\\"pp.3-4\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-fatalism-time-course--e05f6b73a8c2\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Fatalism Time Course\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The psychedelic ceremony was associated with increases in Fatalistic determinism at 4 weeks versus baseline (t(121) = 2.81, p = 0.012, d = 0.25, 95% CI [0.06, 0.37]); however, this effect did not persist at 6 months. For psychedelic-naive participants, the 6-month effect persisted (t(52) = 2.86, p = 0.012, d = 0.39, 95% CI [0.11, 0.64]).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This result separates fatalism from the more durable non-physicalist belief changes. It shows that psychedelic-associated belief change is not uniform across metaphysical domains and that subgroup history matters: the overall sample shows a temporary fatalism increase, while first-time users show a longer-lasting one.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"1\\\", \\\"4\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Supplementary Fig. 2a\\\", \\\"p.3\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-wellbeing-association--f16a7c84b9d3\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Well-Being Association\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"A significant positive correlation was found between shifts away from hard-materialism (the NPB factor) and changes in well-being. The correlation was significant at 4 weeks and at 6 months post-ceremony. Figure 3 states that shifts away from hard materialistic beliefs are associated with increases in well-being.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This figure-level and text-level evidence connects metaphysical belief change to psychological outcome, making the findings relevant beyond abstract belief measurement. The evidence establishes association, not whether non-physicalist belief change causes well-being improvement or whether both reflect a wider therapeutic process.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"figure_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"5\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 3\\\", \\\"p.5\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-emotional-synchrony-pathway--a27b8d95c0e4\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Emotional Synchrony Pathway\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Path analysis supported a model with excellent fit in which perceived emotional synchrony with other participants—moderated by baseline peer conformity—predicted subsequent changes in the NPB factor. Acute emotional synchrony was itself predicted by trait absorption, gender, age, baseline beliefs, and identity fusion with the ceremony group.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The path model provides a candidate mechanism for belief change in ceremonial settings. It suggests that psychedelic effects operate through social and contextual experience, not only through isolated drug exposure. Because the model is observational, it should be read as mechanistic evidence for plausibility rather than proof of a single causal chain.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"model_constraint\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"6\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 4\\\", \\\"p.5\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-controlled-psilocybin-comparison--b38c9ea6d1f5\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Controlled Psilocybin Comparison\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"In the double-blind randomized trial, a significant drug versus time interaction was observed (F(56) = 3.13, p = 0.041, one-tailed). Shifts away from hard materialism were evident in the psilocybin group only (Z = 2.28, p = 0.02, d = 0.45), while the escitalopram group showed no changes in NPB (Z = 0.24, p = 0.33, d = 0.2).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The controlled-trial evidence is the strongest design element in the paper because it compares psilocybin therapy against an active antidepressant condition. It supports specificity of the belief shift to psilocybin in this depression-treatment sample, though the trial is smaller than the observational survey and differs in population and context.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"7\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 5a\\\", \\\"p.5\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-psilocybin-responder-shift--c49dab17e2a6\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Responder Belief Shift\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Greater shifts away from hard materialistic beliefs were found for patients with a clinically meaningful response to psilocybin therapy, defined as at least 50% reduction in depression scores from baseline to week 6, compared with responders to escitalopram (Z = 1.74, p = 0.041, g = 0.56, 90% CI [− 0.17, 1.26]).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This result links metaphysical belief change to clinically meaningful improvement within the controlled depression study. It complements the ceremony cohort’s well-being association, but it still leaves open whether belief change is a driver, marker, or consequence of clinical response.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"8\\\", \\\"5\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 5b\\\", \\\"p.5\\\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"2\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"9\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Larger effects among first-time psychedelic users strengthen the inference that new psychedelic exposure contributes to belief change.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"6\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"9\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"A plausible social-experiential pathway makes the causal interpretation more coherent while preserving contextual limits.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"7\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"9\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The randomized psilocybin comparison independently reproduces the predicted movement away from hard materialism.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"derived_from\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"1\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Durability at six months is derived from the same non-physicalist belief shift measured prospectively.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"similar\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"5\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"8\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Both claims connect movement away from materialism with positive mental-health or clinical-response outcomes.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Timmermann and colleagues report that psychedelic use is associated with shifts away from hard materialist metaphysical beliefs toward non-physicalist views, with convergent evidence from a prospective ceremony survey and a smaller controlled psilocybin therapy trial. The strongest results concern non-physicalist belief change, persistence to six months, mediation by emotional synchrony, and association with improved well-being or depression response, while publication date metadata could not be resolved from the local record.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"missing_publication_date\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Publication date could not be resolved from the provided source record; source_date_inference.date is null.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"contextual_causality_limit\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"The observational ceremony data are prospective but remain vulnerable to ceremony culture, expectancy, sample-selection, and unmeasured religious or metaphysical-interest confounds.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"This Scientific Reports article examines whether psychedelics change metaphysical beliefs. It reports shifts away from hard materialism toward non-physicalist views, supported by a ceremony survey and a smaller psilocybin trial. The findings are linked to emotional synchrony and mental-health improvement but remain context-limited.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"The source investigates a question often asserted in psychedelic culture but rarely tested quantitatively: whether psychedelic experiences alter deep metaphysical commitments. The authors use a large prospective online survey of people planning guided or ceremonial psychedelic experiences, measuring beliefs before the event and again afterward, and they supplement this with data from a randomized controlled depression trial comparing psilocybin therapy with escitalopram. The main construct is a Non-physicalist Beliefs factor built from questionnaire items about supernatural realms, non-natural unifying principles, panpsychism, dualism, idealism, materialism, and naturalism. Across the ceremony sample, scores shift significantly away from hard physicalism at four weeks and remain shifted at six months, with larger effects in psychedelic-naive participants. Fatalistic determinism also rises after ceremonies, but its durability is weaker in the overall sample. The paper further reports that baseline lifetime psychedelic exposure correlates with stronger panpsychist and weaker materialist views, that shifts away from hard materialism correlate with improved well-being, and that a path model identifies perceived emotional synchrony during the ceremony as a mediator of later non-physicalist belief change. The controlled trial adds an important check: psilocybin therapy, but not escitalopram, is associated with movement away from hard materialism at six weeks, and psilocybin responders show greater shifts than escitalopram responders. The authors therefore argue that psychedelic use may causally influence metaphysical beliefs, but they do not treat the causal case as settled. They flag limitations including atypical, largely Western and well-educated samples, missing religious-affiliation and environmental-susceptibility measures, and incomplete measurement of the cultural milieu around ceremonies and therapy. The practical upshot is both scientific and ethical: psychedelic experiences may affect core beliefs, and informed consent in psychedelic services may need to mention that possibility.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Central Argument\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper’s central argument is that psychedelic use can move people away from hard physicalist or materialist metaphysical positions. This is not framed as a purely philosophical thesis; it is operationalized through questionnaire items and tested before and after psychedelic exposure, then compared with a controlled psilocybin therapy dataset.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"7\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Measurement Strategy\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The main measurement device is a Metaphysical Beliefs Questionnaire whose items are reduced to a Non-physicalist Beliefs factor. The factor’s positive side includes supernatural realms, non-natural unifying principles, panpsychism, dualism, and idealism, while its negative side includes hard materialist and naturalist commitments. This makes the results interpretable as movement along a materialist-to-non-physicalist axis rather than a single-item endorsement change.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Prospective Findings\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The ceremony cohort supplies the main temporal evidence: respondents’ non-physicalist scores increase four weeks after psychedelic use and remain elevated at six months. The effect is stronger among psychedelic-naive participants, which supports the possibility that new exposure matters rather than only pre-existing psychedelic-culture affiliation.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"1\\\", \\\"2\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Belief Domains\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The belief changes are not uniform. Panpsychist, dualist, transcendentalist, and related non-physicalist tendencies show durability, while fatalistic determinism rises more transiently in the overall sample. This distinction matters because it suggests psychedelics may alter several metaphysical domains differently, depending on cultural reinforcement, prior exposure, and interpretive context.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"1\\\", \\\"3\\\", \\\"4\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Mechanism and Context\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The path model emphasizes social experience during the ceremony. Perceived emotional synchrony predicts later non-physicalist belief change, with peer conformity moderating the pathway and traits such as absorption, age, gender, baseline beliefs, and identity fusion feeding into synchrony. The source therefore treats psychedelic belief change as a drug-context interaction rather than an isolated pharmacological effect.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"6\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Mental-Health Link\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper connects metaphysical movement away from hard materialism with better mental-health outcomes. In the ceremony data, such shifts correlate with well-being improvements at four weeks and six months. In the controlled depression trial, psilocybin responders show larger belief shifts than escitalopram responders, raising the possibility that belief flexibility, spiritual interpretation, or related therapeutic processes accompany clinical improvement.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"5\\\", \\\"8\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Limits and Ethics\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The authors stress that the samples are not demographically or culturally neutral and that religious affiliation, environmental susceptibility, and surrounding metaphysical views were not fully measured. These limits temper causal certainty but also sharpen the ethical issue: if psychedelics can catalyze changes in core beliefs, participants and service users may need to be informed of that possibility before exposure.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"9\\\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n\nwith output_json_path.open('w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    json.dump(data, f, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)\n    f.write('\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\noutput_json_path = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/runs/run-20260426T152518Z--g2jrtnjpv1/semantic/ingest_extraction.json')\noutput_json_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\ndata = {\n  \\\"source_date_inference\\\": {\n    \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\n    \\\"date\\\": None,\n    \\\"origin\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\n    \\\"rationale\\\": None\n  },\n  \\\"source\\\": {\n    \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n    \\\"display_title\\\": \\\"Psychedelics Shift Beliefs Beyond Hard Materialism\\\",\n    \\\"authors\\\": [\n      \\\"Christopher Timmermann\\\",\n      \\\"Hannes Kettner\\\",\n      \\\"Chris Letheby\\\",\n      \\\"Leor Roseman\\\",\n      \\\"Fernando E. Rosas\\\",\n      \\\"Robin L. Carhart-Harris\\\"\n    ],\n    \\\"doi\\\": \\\"10.1038/s41598-021-01209-2\\\",\n    \\\"journal\\\": \\\"Scientific Reports\\\"\n  },\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Psychedelic use is associated with significant shifts away from hard physicalist or materialist metaphysical beliefs and toward non-physicalist positions such as panpsychism, dualism, and non-naturalism.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Psychedelics reduce hard materialism\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Pooling scores for the NPB factor, analyses revealed a significant shift away from physicalism at 4 weeks compared with baseline (t(121) = 3.66, p = 0.001, d = 0.33, 95% CI [0.12, 0.39]).\\\",\n        \\\"The Non-Physicalist Beliefs factor included positive loadings for beliefs in supernatural realms, non-natural unifying principles, panpsychism, dualism, and solipsism/idealism, while materialism and naturalism loaded negatively.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Most psychedelic-associated shifts in non-physicalist metaphysical beliefs persisted for at least six months, while fatalistic determinism was less durable in the overall observational sample.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Belief shifts partly endure\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"These changes were sustained 6 months after the ceremony (t(121) = 5.07, p < 0.0001, d = 0.46, 95% CI [0.22, 0.50]).\\\",\n        \\\"The observed changes were enduring, persisting for up to 6 months in most domains, with the exception of fatalistic determinism.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Participants with no prior psychedelic experience showed larger shifts away from physicalism than the full observational sample.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Naive users shift more\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Their first psychedelic experience ... significant changes found at 4 weeks (t(52) = 3.85, p = 0.001, d = 0.53, 95% CI [0.21, 0.66]) and 6 months (t(52) = 5.32, p < 0.0001, d = 0.73, 95% CI [0.36, 0.80]).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Greater lifetime psychedelic use correlates with stronger baseline panpsychist beliefs and weaker baseline hard materialist beliefs.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Past use predicts panpsychism\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"A significant positive correlation was found between previous psychedelic use and shifts away from the hard-materialism pole of the hard-materialism vs. hard-dualism spectrum at baseline (r = 0.223, p < 0.0001).\\\",\n        \\\"Lifetime psychedelic use was positively correlated with panpsychist views and negatively correlated with hard materialistic views measured at baseline.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Ceremonial psychedelic use increased fatalistic determinism at four weeks in the overall prospective sample, but this effect did not persist to six months except in the psychedelic-naive subgroup.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Fatalism rises temporarily\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"The psychedelic ceremony was associated with increases in scores of Fatalistic determinism at 4 weeks versus baseline (t(121) = 2.81, p = 0.012, d = 0.25, 95% CI [0.06, 0.37]); however, this effect did not persist at 6 months.\\\",\n        \\\"For psychedelic-naïve participants ... the changes persisted for at least 6 months (t(52) = 2.86, p = 0.012, d = 0.39, 95% CI [0.11, 0.64]).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Shifts away from hard materialism are positively associated with increases in psychological well-being after psychedelic ceremonies.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Belief change tracks wellbeing\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"A significant positive correlation was found between shifts away from hard-materialism (the NPB factor) and changes in well-being. The correlation was significant at 4 weeks and at 6 months post-ceremony.\\\",\n        \\\"Figure 3. Shifts away from hard materialistic beliefs are associated with increases in well-being.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Perceived emotional synchrony during the psychedelic ceremony mediates later changes in non-physicalist beliefs, with baseline peer conformity moderating that pathway.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Synchrony mediates belief change\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Results supported a model with excellent fit in which perceived ‘emotional synchrony’ with other participants—moderated by baseline scores of peer conformity—predicted subsequent changes in the NPB factor.\\\",\n        \\\"Figure 4. Changes in non-physicalist beliefs are moderated by baseline variables and pre-state identify fusion and mediated by acute emotional synchrony during the psychedelic session.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"In a randomized controlled depression trial, psilocybin therapy produced shifts away from hard materialism at six weeks whereas escitalopram did not.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Psilocybin shifts materialism\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"A significant drug versus time interaction was observed (F(56) = 3.13, p = 0.041, one-tailed).\\\",\n        \\\"Post-hoc tests reveal that shifts away from hard materialism were evident in the psilocybin group only (Z = 2.28, p = 0.02, d = 0.45). The escitalopram group showed no changes in NPB (Z = 0.24, p = 0.33, d = 0.2).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Patients who clinically responded to psilocybin therapy showed greater shifts away from hard materialist beliefs than patients who responded to escitalopram.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Psilocybin response shifts beliefs\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Significantly greater shifts away from hard materialistic beliefs were found for those patients who showed a clinically meaningful response to psilocybin only ... versus those who showed a response to escitalopram (Z = 1.74, p = 0.041, g = 0.56, 90% CI [− 0.17, 1.26]).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"The combined prospective, correlational, mechanistic, and controlled-trial evidence supports a possible causal influence of psychedelic use on metaphysical belief change, while remaining limited by sample selection and contextual confounds.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Causality remains context-limited\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"These lines of evidence include: (1) that the belief-shifts were observed prospectively ... (5) independent data from a separate controlled study in a different population, replicated our observational research findings.\\\",\n        \\\"The study did not collect respondents’ and patients’ religious affiliations at baseline, differential environmental susceptibilities, or interests concerning metaphysical beliefs.\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"evidence_items\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-nonphysicalist-factor-loadings--a15b9c27d4e8\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Non-Physicalist Factor Structure\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"A factor analysis on the belief items revealed a single Non-physicalist Beliefs factor with good internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha = 0.86). Positive loadings included separate and supernatural realms, a non-natural unifying principle, panpsychism, dualism, and solipsism/idealism; negative loadings included hard materialism, physicalism, and naturalism.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This factor-analysis result defines the main outcome variable used throughout the study. It matters because the reported belief changes are not tied to a single questionnaire item, but to a composite that contrasts non-physicalist positions with hard materialist or naturalist positions. The interpretation remains bounded by the authors’ warning that philosophical labels for items are contestable.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"0\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Table 1\\\", \\\"p.2\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-prospective-npb-shift--b621cf90a43d\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Prospective Belief Shift\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Pooling scores for the NPB factor, analyses revealed a significant shift away from physicalism at 4 weeks compared with baseline (t(121) = 3.66, p = 0.001, d = 0.33, 95% CI [0.12, 0.39]). These changes were sustained 6 months after the ceremony (t(121) = 5.07, p < 0.0001, d = 0.46, 95% CI [0.22, 0.50]).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The pre-versus-post comparison is the central prospective evidence that psychedelic ceremonies were followed by measurable movement away from hard physicalism. It establishes temporal ordering and durability for the composite non-physicalist belief measure, though the observational ceremony context means it does not by itself fully isolate the pharmacological cause.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"1\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 1\\\", \\\"p.2\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-naive-participant-effects--c83d4f51e6a0\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Naive Participant Effects\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"For respondents embarking on their first psychedelic experience, significant changes were found at 4 weeks (t(52) = 3.85, p = 0.001, d = 0.53, 95% CI [0.21, 0.66]) and 6 months (t(52) = 5.32, p < 0.0001, d = 0.73, 95% CI [0.36, 0.80]).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The stronger effect sizes among psychedelic-naive participants support the interpretation that the observed shifts are not merely a residue of earlier psychedelic exposure. This evidence is especially relevant to causal interpretation, while still sharing the observational limitations of the ceremony cohort.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"2\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Supplementary Fig. 1a\\\", \\\"p.3\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-lifetime-use-correlations--d94e5a62f7b1\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Lifetime Use Correlations\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"A significant positive correlation was found between previous psychedelic use and shifts away from the hard-materialism pole of the hard-materialism vs. hard-dualism spectrum at baseline (r = 0.223, p < 0.0001). Figure 2 reports that lifetime psychedelic use was positively correlated with panpsychist views and negatively correlated with hard materialistic views measured at baseline.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The cross-sectional baseline correlation gives convergent evidence that psychedelic exposure and metaphysical orientation vary together even before the prospective ceremony interval. It is not sufficient for causation, but it reinforces the direction of the prospective findings and specifically links past exposure to panpsychist and anti-materialist endorsement.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"3\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 1\\\", \\\"Fig. 2\\\", \\\"pp.3-4\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-fatalism-time-course--e05f6b73a8c2\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Fatalism Time Course\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The psychedelic ceremony was associated with increases in Fatalistic determinism at 4 weeks versus baseline (t(121) = 2.81, p = 0.012, d = 0.25, 95% CI [0.06, 0.37]); however, this effect did not persist at 6 months. For psychedelic-naive participants, the 6-month effect persisted (t(52) = 2.86, p = 0.012, d = 0.39, 95% CI [0.11, 0.64]).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This result separates fatalism from the more durable non-physicalist belief changes. It shows that psychedelic-associated belief change is not uniform across metaphysical domains and that subgroup history matters: the overall sample shows a temporary fatalism increase, while first-time users show a longer-lasting one.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"1\\\", \\\"4\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Supplementary Fig. 2a\\\", \\\"p.3\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-wellbeing-association--f16a7c84b9d3\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Well-Being Association\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"A significant positive correlation was found between shifts away from hard-materialism (the NPB factor) and changes in well-being. The correlation was significant at 4 weeks and at 6 months post-ceremony. Figure 3 states that shifts away from hard materialistic beliefs are associated with increases in well-being.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This figure-level and text-level evidence connects metaphysical belief change to psychological outcome, making the findings relevant beyond abstract belief measurement. The evidence establishes association, not whether non-physicalist belief change causes well-being improvement or whether both reflect a wider therapeutic process.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"figure_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"5\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 3\\\", \\\"p.5\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-emotional-synchrony-pathway--a27b8d95c0e4\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Emotional Synchrony Pathway\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Path analysis supported a model with excellent fit in which perceived emotional synchrony with other participants—moderated by baseline peer conformity—predicted subsequent changes in the NPB factor. Acute emotional synchrony was itself predicted by trait absorption, gender, age, baseline beliefs, and identity fusion with the ceremony group.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The path model provides a candidate mechanism for belief change in ceremonial settings. It suggests that psychedelic effects operate through social and contextual experience, not only through isolated drug exposure. Because the model is observational, it should be read as mechanistic evidence for plausibility rather than proof of a single causal chain.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"model_constraint\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"6\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 4\\\", \\\"p.5\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-controlled-psilocybin-comparison--b38c9ea6d1f5\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Controlled Psilocybin Comparison\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"In the double-blind randomized trial, a significant drug versus time interaction was observed (F(56) = 3.13, p = 0.041, one-tailed). Shifts away from hard materialism were evident in the psilocybin group only (Z = 2.28, p = 0.02, d = 0.45), while the escitalopram group showed no changes in NPB (Z = 0.24, p = 0.33, d = 0.2).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The controlled-trial evidence is the strongest design element in the paper because it compares psilocybin therapy against an active antidepressant condition. It supports specificity of the belief shift to psilocybin in this depression-treatment sample, though the trial is smaller than the observational survey and differs in population and context.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"7\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 5a\\\", \\\"p.5\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-psilocybin-responder-shift--c49dab17e2a6\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Responder Belief Shift\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Greater shifts away from hard materialistic beliefs were found for patients with a clinically meaningful response to psilocybin therapy, defined as at least 50% reduction in depression scores from baseline to week 6, compared with responders to escitalopram (Z = 1.74, p = 0.041, g = 0.56, 90% CI [− 0.17, 1.26]).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This result links metaphysical belief change to clinically meaningful improvement within the controlled depression study. It complements the ceremony cohort’s well-being association, but it still leaves open whether belief change is a driver, marker, or consequence of clinical response.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"8\\\", \\\"5\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-04-psychedelics-alter-metaphysical-beliefs--0d1ce18240f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 5b\\\", \\\"p.5\\\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"2\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"9\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Larger effects among first-time psychedelic users strengthen the inference that new psychedelic exposure contributes to belief change.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"6\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"9\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"A plausible social-experiential pathway makes the causal interpretation more coherent while preserving contextual limits.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"7\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"9\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The randomized psilocybin comparison independently reproduces the predicted movement away from hard materialism.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"derived_from\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"1\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Durability at six months is derived from the same non-physicalist belief shift measured prospectively.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"similar\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"5\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"8\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Both claims connect movement away from materialism with positive mental-health or clinical-response outcomes.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Timmermann and colleagues report that psychedelic use is associated with shifts away from hard materialist metaphysical beliefs toward non-physicalist views, with convergent evidence from a prospective ceremony survey and a smaller controlled psilocybin therapy trial. The strongest results concern non-physicalist belief change, persistence to six months, mediation by emotional synchrony, and association with improved well-being or depression response, while publication date metadata could not be resolved from the local record.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"missing_publication_date\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Publication date could not be resolved from the provided source record; source_date_inference.date is null.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"contextual_causality_limit\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"The observational ceremony data are prospective but remain vulnerable to ceremony culture, expectancy, sample-selection, and unmeasured religious or metaphysical-interest confounds.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"This Scientific Reports article examines whether psychedelics change metaphysical beliefs. It reports shifts away from hard materialism toward non-physicalist views, supported by a ceremony survey and a smaller psilocybin trial. The findings are linked to emotional synchrony and mental-health improvement but remain context-limited.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"The source investigates a question often asserted in psychedelic culture but rarely tested quantitatively: whether psychedelic experiences alter deep metaphysical commitments. The authors use a large prospective online survey of people planning guided or ceremonial psychedelic experiences, measuring beliefs before the event and again afterward, and they supplement this with data from a randomized controlled depression trial comparing psilocybin therapy with escitalopram. The main construct is a Non-physicalist Beliefs factor built from questionnaire items about supernatural realms, non-natural unifying principles, panpsychism, dualism, idealism, materialism, and naturalism. Across the ceremony sample, scores shift significantly away from hard physicalism at four weeks and remain shifted at six months, with larger effects in psychedelic-naive participants. Fatalistic determinism also rises after ceremonies, but its durability is weaker in the overall sample. The paper further reports that baseline lifetime psychedelic exposure correlates with stronger panpsychist and weaker materialist views, that shifts away from hard materialism correlate with improved well-being, and that a path model identifies perceived emotional synchrony during the ceremony as a mediator of later non-physicalist belief change. The controlled trial adds an important check: psilocybin therapy, but not escitalopram, is associated with movement away from hard materialism at six weeks, and psilocybin responders show greater shifts than escitalopram responders. The authors therefore argue that psychedelic use may causally influence metaphysical beliefs, but they do not treat the causal case as settled. They flag limitations including atypical, largely Western and well-educated samples, missing religious-affiliation and environmental-susceptibility measures, and incomplete measurement of the cultural milieu around ceremonies and therapy. The practical upshot is both scientific and ethical: psychedelic experiences may affect core beliefs, and informed consent in psychedelic services may need to mention that possibility.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Central Argument\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper’s central argument is that psychedelic use can move people away from hard physicalist or materialist metaphysical positions. This is not framed as a purely philosophical thesis; it is operationalized through questionnaire items and tested before and after psychedelic exposure, then compared with a controlled psilocybin therapy dataset.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"7\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Measurement Strategy\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The main measurement device is a Metaphysical Beliefs Questionnaire whose items are reduced to a Non-physicalist Beliefs factor. The factor’s positive side includes supernatural realms, non-natural unifying principles, panpsychism, dualism, and idealism, while its negative side includes hard materialist and naturalist commitments. This makes the results interpretable as movement along a materialist-to-non-physicalist axis rather than a single-item endorsement change.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Prospective Findings\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The ceremony cohort supplies the main temporal evidence: respondents’ non-physicalist scores increase four weeks after psychedelic use and remain elevated at six months. The effect is stronger among psychedelic-naive participants, which supports the possibility that new exposure matters rather than only pre-existing psychedelic-culture affiliation.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"1\\\", \\\"2\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Belief Domains\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The belief changes are not uniform. Panpsychist, dualist, transcendentalist, and related non-physicalist tendencies show durability, while fatalistic determinism rises more transiently in the overall sample. This distinction matters because it suggests psychedelics may alter several metaphysical domains differently, depending on cultural reinforcement, prior exposure, and interpretive context.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"1\\\", \\\"3\\\", \\\"4\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Mechanism and Context\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The path model emphasizes social experience during the ceremony. Perceived emotional synchrony predicts later non-physicalist belief change, with peer conformity moderating the pathway and traits such as absorption, age, gender, baseline beliefs, and identity fusion feeding into synchrony. The source therefore treats psychedelic belief change as a drug-context interaction rather than an isolated pharmacological effect.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"6\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Mental-Health Link\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper connects metaphysical movement away from hard materialism with better mental-health outcomes. In the ceremony data, such shifts correlate with well-being improvements at four weeks and six months. In the controlled depression trial, psilocybin responders show larger belief shifts than escitalopram responders, raising the possibility that belief flexibility, spiritual interpretation, or related therapeutic processes accompany clinical improvement.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"5\\\", \\\"8\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Limits and Ethics\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The authors stress that the samples are not demographically or culturally neutral and that religious affiliation, environmental susceptibility, and surrounding metaphysical views were not fully measured. These limits temper causal certainty but also sharpen the ethical issue: if psychedelics can catalyze changes in core beliefs, participants and service users may need to be informed of that possibility before exposure.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"9\\\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n\nwith output_json_path.open('w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    json.dump(data, f, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)\n    f.write('\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
