    "summary_short": "This Scientific Reports article examines whether psychedelics change metaphysical beliefs. It reports shifts away from hard materialism toward non-physicalist views, supported by a ceremony survey and a smaller psilocybin trial. The findings are linked to emotional synchrony and mental-health improvement but remain context-limited.",
    "summary_long": "The source investigates a question often asserted in psychedelic culture but rarely tested quantitatively: whether psychedelic experiences alter deep metaphysical commitments. The authors use a large prospective online survey of people planning guided or ceremonial psychedelic experiences, measuring beliefs before the event and again afterward, and they supplement this with data from a randomized controlled depression trial comparing psilocybin therapy with escitalopram. The main construct is a Non-physicalist Beliefs factor built from questionnaire items about supernatural realms, non-natural unifying principles, panpsychism, dualism, idealism, materialism, and naturalism. Across the ceremony sample, scores shift significantly away from hard physicalism at four weeks and remain shifted at six months, with larger effects in psychedelic-naive participants. Fatalistic determinism also rises after ceremonies, but its durability is weaker in the overall sample. The paper further reports that baseline lifetime psychedelic exposure correlates with stronger panpsychist and weaker materialist views, that shifts away from hard materialism correlate with improved well-being, and that a path model identifies perceived emotional synchrony during the ceremony as a mediator of later non-physicalist belief change. The controlled trial adds an important check: psilocybin therapy, but not escitalopram, is associated with movement away from hard materialism at six weeks, and psilocybin responders show greater shifts than escitalopram responders. The authors therefore argue that psychedelic use may causally influence metaphysical beliefs, but they do not treat the causal case as settled. They flag limitations including atypical, largely Western and well-educated samples, missing religious-affiliation and environmental-susceptibility measures, and incomplete measurement of the cultural milieu around ceremonies and therapy. The practical upshot is both scientific and ethical: psychedelic experiences may affect core beliefs, and informed consent in psychedelic services may need to mention that possibility.",
